    "explanation": "To both parties. The route question is settled by the GPS trace, not by the trip record's rounded copy. EV-GPS-00505 measures 30.89 km between the recorded pickup and dropoff; the platform's optimal estimate stored on EV-TRIP-00505 is 22.10 km. The excess is 8.79 km, or 39.8% of optimal, which clears both POL-RD-01 establishment tests (over 20% and over 0.8 km) and also the 8.0 km absolute gate, so this is a MATERIAL deviation. The trace is complete — 217 fixes at a 10-second cadence, 100% coverage, no gaps and no jump — so it is not impaired under POL-RD-07, nothing need corroborate it, and no POL-RD-10(3) escalation arises. The driver's own statement ('I went around') does not dispute the route. The driver's fog account does not excuse the deviation. POL-RD-03 exonerates only against a traffic advisory naming a road the optimal route uses, with a window covering the trip. The only advisory in the record is EV-ADVIS-00505-01, which is MISSING — no payload, weight 0.0, issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT — and it records that no advisory covered the SLE or TPE in that window. The driver produced no advisory ID and no artifact of his own, so POL-RD-08 applies: a general assertion that traffic was bad, unsupported by a traffic_advisory ID, does not discharge the burden that shifts to the driver once the rider has shown a qualifying excess on an unimpaired trace. The fog assertion is discarded and the excess stands. I have considered, and do not adopt, the submission that this record must be escalated under POL-RD-10(5). That trigger covers an exculpatory clause invoked against a cited artifact that is missing from the pool, does not cover the trip window, or lacks the field the clause requires. Here the driver cited no artifact and invoked no clause in reliance on one; the record is not missing a document it should hold (LTA issued no advisory for that corridor and window, as the mined artifact itself records), so the POL-RD-03 test is not untestable — it runs and fails. That is a failure of the driver's proof, which POL-RD-08 resolves, not a broken or incomplete record of the kind POL-RD-10 holds. The refund is arithmetic. The fare is METERED on EV-FARE-00505, so POL-RD-02 governs and the upfront-quote route in POL-RD-11 does not. Recomputing on the optimal route: ((3.80 base fare + 0.75 × 22.10 km + 0.28 × 27.0 min) × surge 1.0, read from the fare breakdown's own surge_multiplier) + 0.80 platform fee = 28.735. Charged total was 37.86. Refund = 37.86 − 28.735 = 9.125, rounded up to SGD 9.15. That is 24.2% of the charge, well below the 90% that would make it a full refund and well above the SGD 0.50 de minimis, so the outcome is PARTIAL_REFUND. No other money moves. There is no off-route ERP, toll or surcharge line to correct (all nil), so POL-RD-09 adds nothing and its anti-double-count rule keeps the distance refund clean. The driver's trailing-90-day record (0 deviation flags in 250 completed trips) establishes no POL-RD-06 pattern, so there is no full-refund uplift and no conduct referral. The chat log is empty, so no rider-requested detour under POL-RD-04; no amendment is stamped, so POL-RD-05 does not run; the vehicle is RYDE_PLUS, so POL-RD-12 does not apply. The SGD 210 rebooking cost and the missed check-in are not remedies this policy provides and no clause turns them into a refundable line; the contested sum is the 37.86 charged. Profile fields other than the two trailing-90-day counters POL-RD-06 names (and the POL-RD-08 rider counters, which are not engaged) are context only under POL-GEN-01 — the driver's 4.86 rating, account age, lifetime trips and lifetime prior_deviation_flags counter, and the rider's own profile — and I have used none of them as a ground. The counterparty statement is evidence, but it carries no weight multiplier and does not enter or move the confidence cap. Confidence is 0.90: the deciding facts are carried by artifacts each at weight 1.0 (EV-GPS-00505, EV-TRIP-00505, EV-FARE-00505), so nothing caps it below the floor."
